WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.259 --> 00:00:02.506
-  Aaron Rodgers  

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:07.007
You're the greatest
quarterback we've ever seen  

00:00:07.351 --> 00:00:10.744
You've been carrying
this team for a decade  

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:13.213
-Guys, guys,
you've got to start the show.

00:00:13.313 --> 00:00:14.748
-One more. One more.
-One more time!

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:16.483
-  Aaron Rodgers  

00:00:16.583 --> 00:00:18.468
You're the greatest
quarterback we've ever seen  

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:22.956
  

00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:24.324
-Welcome to "Simms &amp; Lefkoe."

00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:26.292
This is Simms. I am Lefkoe.

00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:27.994
Want to say anything?
-Hey, what's up, everybody?

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:29.996
I'm Simms.
-That was awesome.

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:34.334
A packed show today. Simms,
Sean McVay remembering plays.

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:35.902
We got Martellus Bennett
in studio.

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.670
The guy is hilarious.
-Awesome. He's the man.

00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:40.507
-And we have an incredible story
of DeAngelo Hall

00:00:40.607 --> 00:00:43.910
with Paris Hilton
and that guy from 'Baywatch.'

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.311
-Oh, David Hasselhoff
is his name.

00:00:45.412 --> 00:00:46.746
-Thank you.
-Don't worry. I'll help you out.

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:48.581
Keep going there.
Limp along. Keep going.

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:50.617
-This is why people think we're
scripted, 'cuz  you tell --

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:51.851
"This is why
we're gonna do this."

00:00:52.798 --> 00:00:53.937
But we were -- We really do love

00:00:53.934 --> 00:00:55.355
Aaron Rodgers
with all of our heart.

00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:57.252
-Yes.
-Can you explain to people

00:00:57.257 --> 00:01:00.827
how hard it is to do what he did
on Sunday with that leg?

00:01:00.927 --> 00:01:02.622
-I mean, it's something
I look at and go,

00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:04.560
"Lefkoe, there's only
a few quarterbacks in the

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:06.666
history of the sport that
I think could play with that."

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:08.535
That was phenomenal,
let alone to be able

00:01:08.635 --> 00:01:11.504
to get the amount
of power and velocity

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:15.141
on the ball that's needed,
like the Geronimo Allison bomb.

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:17.243
-It was 52 yards in the air
with a bum leg.

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:18.645
-On one leg, yes.

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:20.880
-We just saw Rodgers
get hurt last year.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:22.315
Is it worth the risk?

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:24.517
Why is he not thinking
about being injured?

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:26.286
How does he handle that?
-I think this is one

00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:28.988
of the things that goes
untalked about in the sport.

00:01:29.089 --> 00:01:31.658
You know why he can go,
"I'm playing, no matter what"?

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:34.194
'Cuz he just got
long-term security.

00:01:34.294 --> 00:01:35.462
Why does nobody talk about that?

00:01:35.562 --> 00:01:36.996
-'Cuz he just
got the contract.

00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:38.798
-He got a huge contract,
so now he goes,

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:40.400
"Okay, the team's backing me,

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.735
and I'm gonna do everything
to prove them right

00:01:42.836 --> 00:01:45.071
by giving me all this money,
and I know they gave me

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:47.073
all this money
to be the leader of the team.

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:49.042
And I'm gonna go out there
and show everybody,

00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:51.144
even though I'm hurt,
I can make it happen."

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:52.745
-I'm so happy they paid
Aaron Rodgers.

00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:54.647
-  Oh, Aaron Rodgers  

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:56.816
-  You're the greatest
quarterback we've ever seen  

00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:58.918
-Simms, it is time for...

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:00.987
-Whoa!
-..."Big Take."

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:02.288
-The internet's funny, man.

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:04.190
Something happens
in a football game,

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.493
they jump all over it, like our
boy Sam Darnold threw the pick.

00:02:06.593 --> 00:02:08.495
-Right. Everybody's experts.
-"He was the worst ever!"

00:02:08.595 --> 00:02:10.196
-They're experts.
-So, everybody has a slant.

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:11.731
We're here to straighten it out.

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:14.834
And I saw a lot of attack
on Big Ben on Sunday.

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:16.436
And I get it.
Had a lot of picks.

00:02:16.536 --> 00:02:18.104
Here are some
of the takes, Simms.

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:20.006
I want to straighten them out.
First one --

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:21.641
"As a Steelers fan,
I'm officially over

00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:24.744
the Big Ben Roethlisberger era.
Shake my head."

00:02:24.844 --> 00:02:28.715
This one -- "Big Ben
Roethlisberger, is that you?"

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:30.617
A floating dumpster
down the river.

00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:32.819
-Ohh.
-And, then, we even had, like,

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:34.687
a more official article.

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:37.157
"Ben Roethlisberger's five
turnovers cost the Steelers

00:02:37.257 --> 00:02:38.992
big-time in tie with Browns."

00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:41.828
-Oh, okay.
-Um, I feel like you don't...

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:44.898
-Thank you, Captain Obvious.
-Where do you stand

00:02:44.998 --> 00:02:47.000
on the "Big Ben is trash,
he's washed, it's over"?

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.002
-[ sarcastically ]
Oh, yeah, I know. He's trash.

00:02:49.102 --> 00:02:52.338
I mean, he only was fifth in
touchdowns last year in the NFL.

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:54.607
He only threw for 469 yards
and five touchdowns

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:56.576
against the
best defense in football

00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:58.077
in the divisional playoff game,

00:02:58.178 --> 00:03:00.046
and then, when we get
to this game alone,

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:01.881
okay, he threw
three interceptions.

00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:03.483
Only one was his fault.

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:04.918
One time, there was
a miscommunication

00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:06.286
with Antonio Brown.

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:07.954
Another one went off
Jesse James' head.

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:10.990
Are we gonna blame
Ben Roethlisberger for fumbling

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.359
when the center
snapped the ball on the ground

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:15.328
and then no one blocked
Myles Garrett

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:16.896
as he got blindsided?

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:19.032
I didn't know Ben was
supposed to snap the ball,

00:03:19.132 --> 00:03:21.234
block the left end,
and throw the football.

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:22.602
They should be paying him more.

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:23.903
-And missed the field goal
in overtime.

00:03:24.003 --> 00:03:25.238
-Oh, and missed the --
And wait.

00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:26.906
Wasn't it James Conner
who fumbled

00:03:27.006 --> 00:03:29.164
and they returned the ball
back to the 1-yard line?

00:03:29.261 --> 00:03:32.779
I'm sick of unqualified people
making qualifying statements.

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:34.714
I'm sick of it. Yeah.
-Wow.

00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:37.250
-So, Big Ben...
-He's still really awesome.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.485
He's one of the best
deep-ball throwers in football.

00:03:39.586 --> 00:03:44.174
And let's not forget
it was a monsoon in Cleveland.

00:03:44.174 --> 00:03:45.212
-Oh, yeah.
-Yeah. Oh.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:46.015
I forgot about that.

00:03:46.015 --> 00:03:48.628
And Cleveland's defense is gonna
be one of the best in football.

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:50.230
Okay, next subject. Let's go.

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.465
-I'm actually super-excited
that you're mad,

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:55.068
but we don't like to take
our anger out on the players.

00:03:55.168 --> 00:03:57.170
It's really the coaches' fault
in Week 1.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:58.404
-Yes.
-It's a little segment

00:03:58.504 --> 00:04:00.440
that we like to call "Get 'Em!"

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.075
Where are we starting?

00:04:02.175 --> 00:04:04.143
-Oh, we're starting with
the Falcons' red-zone offense.

00:04:04.244 --> 00:04:06.646
"Oh, we're gonna get the ball
more to Julio Jones this year.

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:09.249
We're gonna be more creative
in the red zone."

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:11.784
Oh, here's their plan for Week 1
against the Eagles.

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:14.153
Straight line, straight line,
straight line.

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:16.322
What the hell were you doing
all offseason?

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:18.524
-Get 'em!
-Oh, hello, Cowboys offense.

00:04:18.625 --> 00:04:20.460
I haven't seen you this year.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.862
What the hell did you do
this offseason?

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:24.864
Did we come up with a game plan

00:04:24.964 --> 00:04:26.833
or anything
schematically creative

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.435
to help out the pass game
or Dak Prescott?

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:31.271
-Probably not. Get 'em!
-Gosh.

00:04:31.371 --> 00:04:33.039
Oh, and the Saints defense --

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:34.841
I don't know what you've been
doing in the offseason

00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:36.409
for the last five years!

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:38.244
You can't win a Week 1 game,

00:04:38.344 --> 00:04:40.813
and everybody was open
against "Fitzmagic."

00:04:40.807 --> 00:04:43.416
I mean, it was the greatest show
I've ever seen,

00:04:43.403 --> 00:04:46.252
and it was the Bucs
and Ryan Fitzpatrick.

00:04:46.352 --> 00:04:48.154
-Get 'em!
-Oh, hello, Seattle.

00:04:48.254 --> 00:04:50.290
"We're gonna protect
Russell Wilson

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:51.858
and help him out more this year.

00:04:51.958 --> 00:04:53.626
It's going --
We're going to Denver,

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:55.862
and, oh, um, uh,
oh, yeah, they have Von Miller.

00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:57.363
We'll just leave him one-on-one

00:04:57.463 --> 00:04:59.732
against our crappy
right tackles all game

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:02.568
and let Russell figure it out
and just run away."

00:05:02.669 --> 00:05:04.370
-Got 'em!
Man, did that feel good?

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.939
-Man, I feel a lot better. Whew!
-This is like therapy for you.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.709
-I had to get that off my chest.
-If there's one coach, though,

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:12.879
that definitely is prepared,
it's Sean McVay.

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:14.614
They won their first game by 20,

00:05:14.714 --> 00:05:16.949
and their team didn't
even play all preseason.

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:20.753
He's so prepared,
he never forgets a thing.

00:05:21.218 --> 00:05:22.288
We saw you on television,

00:05:22.388 --> 00:05:24.308
and you remembered
a ton of plays, okay?

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:25.927
-What do you think
of Sean's brain?

00:05:25.927 --> 00:05:27.060
-What? It's out of this world.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:28.861
It's better than mine.
I can tell you that.

00:05:28.961 --> 00:05:31.698
-Week 12, Saints at Rams.

00:05:31.798 --> 00:05:34.200
4:29 in the second quarter.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.670
2nd and 7 on the Saints' 7.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:40.039
What happens?

00:05:40.489 --> 00:05:41.474
-Oh, Josh Reynolds touchdown

00:05:41.574 --> 00:05:44.043
off scheduled play
versus a 3-man rush.

00:05:44.143 --> 00:05:45.611
-You're absolutely right.
-Are you kidding me?

00:05:45.712 --> 00:05:46.746
-You're unbelievable.
-He's like a computer.

00:05:46.846 --> 00:05:49.248
-Now we are going to go to --

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:52.652
This is Sean McVay's
time in Washington.

00:05:52.752 --> 00:05:54.253
-Oh, no.
-Ooh, I like it.

00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:55.788
-So we're bringing it
back to 2015.

00:05:55.888 --> 00:05:58.658
Week 7. Bucs at Skins.
-Yep.

00:05:58.758 --> 00:06:02.128
-2nd and 7
on the Tampa Bay 24.

00:06:02.228 --> 00:06:04.697
58 seconds left
in the fourth quarter.

00:06:04.987 --> 00:06:07.700
-Jamison Crowder wheel route
down the right sideline.

00:06:07.794 --> 00:06:09.936
-Jamison Crowder wheel route
down the right sideline.

00:06:10.036 --> 00:06:11.938
Set up the first down,
and then how did that drive end?

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:15.386
-Jordan Reed touchdown
in a 4x1 individual iso slant.

00:06:15.386 --> 00:06:17.643
-And what meme was that game?

00:06:17.744 --> 00:06:20.480
-You liked that!
-He's good.

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:22.648
He's real good.
Check out the brain on Sean.

00:06:22.749 --> 00:06:24.417
-You're amazing.
-All right.

00:06:24.747 --> 00:06:29.452
2005 NFC Wild Card Game.
Washington and Tampa Bay.

00:06:29.452 --> 00:06:31.626
-Ah.
-9:47 left in the third.

00:06:31.626 --> 00:06:32.792
-Sean Taylor.

00:06:32.892 --> 00:06:36.590
-3rd and 2 on
the Washington 2-yard line.

00:06:36.655 --> 00:06:38.464
-Oh, well, this is my play.

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:40.032
They're cheating
to make it equal.

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:41.834
They're cheating.
-What's the play?

00:06:41.934 --> 00:06:43.903
-I bootlegged out on the left
and dove in for a touchdown.

00:06:44.003 --> 00:06:45.984
Yeah, it's one of my only...
-Do you remember?

00:06:45.984 --> 00:06:47.073
-My only rushing touchdown.
-Of course.

00:06:47.173 --> 00:06:48.574
-But do you remember?

00:06:48.674 --> 00:06:50.476
-You know what?
Chris was playing with the Bucs.

00:06:50.576 --> 00:06:51.978
That was my first year
in coaching.

00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:53.646
-Yes.
-Yeah.

00:06:53.746 --> 00:06:55.581
We spent a little bit of time.
He won't even remember --

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:57.083
We had some breakfasts
together, you know,

00:06:57.183 --> 00:06:59.336
talked a little ball,
but that was...

00:06:59.336 --> 00:07:00.386
What did he eat at breakfast?

00:07:00.386 --> 00:07:01.298
No, I actually take it back.

00:07:01.298 --> 00:07:02.054
When I worked

00:07:02.054 --> 00:07:03.623
-this was before I coached
for the Bucs...
-yes

00:07:03.723 --> 00:07:05.525
It was when I was
working training camp,

00:07:05.625 --> 00:07:07.527
the year after they
had won the Super Bowl,

00:07:07.627 --> 00:07:08.995
I was working as, like, a --

00:07:09.095 --> 00:07:10.963
You know, being a water boy,
whatever.

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:12.632
-Right, right.
-Just kind of running around.

00:07:12.732 --> 00:07:14.400
-That's awesome. That's awesome.
-Yeah, that's right.

00:07:15.201 --> 00:07:19.024
-Everybody remembers
Todd Gurley's screen

00:07:19.024 --> 00:07:21.207
for a touchdown
against the Cowboys.

00:07:21.307 --> 00:07:24.744
What happened the play
before the big touchdown?

00:07:24.844 --> 00:07:26.112
-Screen to Malcolm Brown.

00:07:26.212 --> 00:07:28.648
-And that is why
he's Sean McVay.

00:07:28.748 --> 00:07:30.349
There's only, like,
a few people, I think,

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:32.585
in the world
that can keep up with us

00:07:32.685 --> 00:07:34.187
and I think are funny,

00:07:34.287 --> 00:07:36.088
and I think Martellus Bennett's
one of those guys.

00:07:36.189 --> 00:07:37.957
-Why, thank you.
I think that's a compliment.

00:07:38.057 --> 00:07:39.559
-Yeah.
-It was a weird compliment.

00:07:39.659 --> 00:07:41.394
-Your brother, too.
He can be there with us, too.

00:07:41.494 --> 00:07:42.829
-Yeah. Oh.
-That would've been good.

00:07:42.929 --> 00:07:44.497
Yeah, you're two
of our favorites.

00:07:44.597 --> 00:07:46.466
-You know, the grape doesn't
fall far from the vine.

00:07:46.566 --> 00:07:48.067
-That's what they say. Yes.
-Yeah.

00:07:48.167 --> 00:07:49.869
-I like, since
it's not a quarterback,

00:07:49.969 --> 00:07:51.635
I get to sit next to him, too.

00:07:51.746 --> 00:07:53.272
-Yeah. Right.
-And, also, I think this

00:07:53.372 --> 00:07:54.407
is the Eagles fans now.
-Well, I don't know.

00:07:54.507 --> 00:07:55.861
-He's officially
an Eagles fan.

00:07:55.861 --> 00:07:57.376
-I am an Eagles fan.
-I hear you.

00:07:57.477 --> 00:07:58.978
-Yeah.
-Jump on the bandwagon.

00:07:59.078 --> 00:08:01.047
-I'm not -- No.
I'm a Michael Bennett fan.

00:08:01.147 --> 00:08:02.882
-Yes. And he plays
for the Eagles.

00:08:02.982 --> 00:08:04.851
-He plays for the Eagles, so
I'm rooting for him to do well,

00:08:04.951 --> 00:08:06.352
so I'm rooting
for them to do well.

00:08:06.452 --> 00:08:08.087
-Yes, okay.
-I still think "Fly, Eagles Fly"

00:08:08.187 --> 00:08:10.743
is the worst song
ever written by anyone,

00:08:10.743 --> 00:08:11.788
next to the national anthem.

00:08:11.791 --> 00:08:13.359
-Right.
-Really?

00:08:13.459 --> 00:08:14.694
-Next to the national anthem.
Okay.

00:08:14.794 --> 00:08:16.329
-Coming out...
-Hardcore!

00:08:16.429 --> 00:08:19.796
-Do you like the "E-A-G-L-E-S"
at the end, though?

00:08:19.796 --> 00:08:21.731
Come on.
-No, I don't like any of it.

00:08:21.731 --> 00:08:23.269
I mean, if I played for them,
I would love it.

00:08:23.369 --> 00:08:24.604
-Perfect. But you're a fan now.

00:08:24.704 --> 00:08:26.439
-It's like saying
if I liked country music,

00:08:26.539 --> 00:08:28.805
I would love country music,
but I don't like country music.

00:08:28.805 --> 00:08:30.476
-What was it like Sunday
being a fan?

00:08:30.576 --> 00:08:31.978
That's, like,
your first time out...

00:08:32.078 --> 00:08:33.412
-I didn't watch
much football Sunday.

00:08:33.513 --> 00:08:35.857
-No?
-What did I do on Sunday?

00:08:35.857 --> 00:08:37.483
Sunday, I --
Oh, I was at Harvard.

00:08:37.583 --> 00:08:40.350
-Excuse me.
-[ laughs ]

00:08:40.372 --> 00:08:42.154
-Our first Sunday
was not the same.

00:08:42.255 --> 00:08:44.487
That's why he's
a lot cooler than me.

00:08:44.487 --> 00:08:46.334
-What do you mean?
-I mean, my first Sunday

00:08:46.334 --> 00:08:48.027
without football, I was a wreck.

00:08:48.127 --> 00:08:49.529
I was crying.
-Really?

00:08:49.629 --> 00:08:51.364
-I was, like,
the exact opposite.

00:08:51.464 --> 00:08:53.366
I was watching every game
and like,

00:08:53.466 --> 00:08:56.168
"I can't believe this guy's
playing and I'm not."

00:08:56.269 --> 00:08:58.791
-I was wondering this, though.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:08:58.791 --> 00:09:00.773
-Is it different if you retire
when you want to retire

00:09:00.873 --> 00:09:02.884
and when the league
pushes you out?

00:09:03.017 --> 00:09:04.530
-I think so,
and they pushed me out.

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:06.287
-See, I retired when I wanted --
I was done.

00:09:06.309 --> 00:09:07.332
-Right. So, again, you're cool.

00:09:07.377 --> 00:09:08.625
-So I'm happy with what I did,
you know?

00:09:08.659 --> 00:09:10.447
A lot of guys --
I think they're --

00:09:10.447 --> 00:09:11.981
I wouldn't say bitter,
necessarily.

00:09:11.981 --> 00:09:13.519
-Right.
-But they're bitter...

00:09:13.619 --> 00:09:15.421
about someone else being in.

00:09:15.521 --> 00:09:17.156
They feel like they still could
do it, they can still go play.

00:09:17.256 --> 00:09:19.579
And then you go try out for
the...

00:09:19.582 --> 00:09:21.075
 -the Canada teams when you're 42.
-No. Never.

00:09:21.075 --> 00:09:21.884
Not happening. 

00:09:21.884 --> 00:09:23.195
-No.
-NFL or nothing.

00:09:23.296 --> 00:09:24.830
-You were talking earlier

00:09:24.931 --> 00:09:27.066
about retiring when you want
and being forced out.

00:09:27.166 --> 00:09:29.902
I feel like I'm looking at Dez,
and I'm seeing the tweets,

00:09:30.002 --> 00:09:32.092
and I can see the frustration.

00:09:32.096 --> 00:09:34.327
And you've been
in that organization.

00:09:34.327 --> 00:09:36.091
What the hell is happening
with Dez right now?

00:09:36.091 --> 00:09:38.557
-These teams can build
perceptions about players 

00:09:38.557 --> 00:09:41.364
Right, and if you take a meeting
and you don't kill

00:09:41.364 --> 00:09:43.607
that perception in that meeting,
they start to believe

00:09:43.635 --> 00:09:44.548
what everyone else has said.
-It becomes reality.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:47.237
-Yeah. It becomes your reality.
-Yeah.

00:09:47.237 --> 00:09:49.350
-So, I think, with Dez,
his thing --

00:09:49.354 --> 00:09:52.311
I think that, you know,
trying to control the situation,

00:09:52.311 --> 00:09:53.921
but, at the same time,
the best thing you can do

00:09:53.921 --> 00:09:55.523
to control the situation
is just chill.

00:09:55.612 --> 00:09:57.210
-Mm.
-Physically, he can still play.

00:09:57.977 --> 00:09:59.398
There's like --
People will sign Eric Decker.

00:09:59.498 --> 00:10:00.866
-Yes.
-Like, you know?

00:10:00.967 --> 00:10:02.844
There's receivers
getting signed all the time.

00:10:02.844 --> 00:10:04.103
-No, the talent's there.

00:10:04.203 --> 00:10:05.504
-Same thing with, like,
an Eric Reid.

00:10:05.605 --> 00:10:07.006
Like, there's safeties
getting signed...

00:10:07.106 --> 00:10:08.708
that can't play better
than Eric Reid.

00:10:08.808 --> 00:10:10.944
It's just like -- But you're
gonna pass up on this guy?

00:10:10.944 --> 00:10:12.278
-End game, though --
how do you see Dez ending --

00:10:12.378 --> 00:10:14.814
Like, end game here? Because
I don't want him to go too long

00:10:14.914 --> 00:10:17.530
without playing here, too,
'cause, like, I like Dez, too.

00:10:17.532 --> 00:10:19.404
I don't think he's
a superstar anymore,

00:10:19.404 --> 00:10:20.860
but I want to see him
on the field,

00:10:20.864 --> 00:10:22.487
and he deserves
to be on the field.

00:10:22.487 --> 00:10:25.441
-I look at teams
that I think would be good.

00:10:25.441 --> 00:10:27.793
I think one team
that comes to mind for me

00:10:27.793 --> 00:10:29.773
that would've been great for him
would've been the Chargers.

00:10:29.773 --> 00:10:30.730
-Mm.
-I think the personality

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:34.074
of the quarterback is gonna make
Dez a better player, right?

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:37.343
So, playing with a Tom Brady
or playing with a...

00:10:37.351 --> 00:10:38.927
-A Philip Rivers, yeah.
-...a Philip Rivers,

00:10:38.927 --> 00:10:41.716
who has that fire
and can match Dez's fire

00:10:41.716 --> 00:10:43.289
and he believes in that fire,

00:10:43.289 --> 00:10:44.968
it's gonna be a better
situation for him,

00:10:44.968 --> 00:10:47.889
because they're gonna have that
mutual respect for one another,

00:10:47.889 --> 00:10:49.641
as far as competitors.

00:10:49.641 --> 00:10:50.922
All these coaches talk about

00:10:50.922 --> 00:10:52.102
they want guys
who love football.

00:10:52.102 --> 00:10:54.946
Like, "Let's get some
football guys in here."

00:10:54.946 --> 00:10:58.685
But when you have a guy who
loves football so much that --

00:10:58.685 --> 00:11:01.143
They label him as a guy
that's like a diva,

00:11:01.143 --> 00:11:03.333
but he just wants to win,
he wants to play,

00:11:03.333 --> 00:11:04.988
and he loves playing football.

00:11:04.996 --> 00:11:06.456
-Right.
-So, if you can get those

00:11:06.463 --> 00:11:07.998
three things out of most guys,
'cause most guys

00:11:07.998 --> 00:11:09.604
don't really love it
the way they say they love it.

00:11:09.604 --> 00:11:11.275
They just say that shit
until they get the check,

00:11:11.275 --> 00:11:12.816
'cause most of these people
aren't who they really say

00:11:12.816 --> 00:11:14.309
they are --
it's just a facade.

00:11:14.313 --> 00:11:15.815
-Yeah, some fugazis.

00:11:15.815 --> 00:11:17.172
-Yeah, they just play
the game very well.

00:11:17.176 --> 00:11:18.705
I wasn't interested
in playing that game.

00:11:18.711 --> 00:11:20.336
I was into playing
the game of football.

00:11:20.678 --> 00:11:22.445
-Martellus,
we appreciate you, man.

00:11:22.445 --> 00:11:25.251
Again, check out Martellus'
show, 'Mostly Football'.

00:11:25.251 --> 00:11:26.484
Yahoo?
-And Complex.

00:11:26.488 --> 00:11:29.332
And my new podcast called
'Revenge of the Jocks'.

00:11:29.332 --> 00:11:31.059
-Almost as many jobs as you.
-Oh, 'Revenge of the Jocks.

00:11:31.059 --> 00:11:32.291
I want to get
a little something here.

00:11:32.391 --> 00:11:34.170
-Oh, man.
-I thought I was the only one

00:11:34.171 --> 00:11:35.784
that was gonna get one.

00:11:35.906 --> 00:11:37.163
-He has a little more flavor
than you.

00:11:37.168 --> 00:11:38.915
You're like --
You know, like --

00:11:38.915 --> 00:11:42.035
-You don't have as much cacao
as he has.
-Yeah!

00:11:42.043 --> 00:11:44.623
He knows white chocolate
when he sees it!

00:11:44.623 --> 00:11:46.051
-I wasn't ready for that.

00:11:46.051 --> 00:11:47.339
[ laughter ]

00:11:48.565 --> 00:11:50.423
-Hey, what's going on, guys?
I'm Reggie Bush.

00:11:50.423 --> 00:11:51.808
And you're watching
"Simms &amp; Lefkoe."

00:11:53.092 --> 00:11:54.560
-So, if you know
"Simms &amp; Lefkoe," you know

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:56.247
that, when he's evaluating
players, you really care

00:11:56.247 --> 00:11:57.788
about two body parts --

00:11:57.796 --> 00:12:00.003
the legs and the ass.
-And the ass, yes.

00:12:00.003 --> 00:12:01.771
-A lot of people
are weirded out,

00:12:01.779 --> 00:12:04.009
but you believe that
if you got those two things,

00:12:04.009 --> 00:12:05.253
you're going to be good.

00:12:05.245 --> 00:12:06.916
-You got a chance.
Lawrence Taylor.

00:12:06.924 --> 00:12:08.538
Reggie White. Barry Sanders.

00:12:08.538 --> 00:12:11.126
You look at their lower halves,
it's pretty impressive,

00:12:11.126 --> 00:12:13.957
and then you can go, "Man.
Hall of Fame type of players."

00:12:13.957 --> 00:12:15.563
-Don't be blinded by the bicep
is what you're saying.

00:12:15.563 --> 00:12:17.502
-That means nothing. Right.

00:12:17.504 --> 00:12:20.140
-So, take me through five guys
at different positions

00:12:20.146 --> 00:12:21.696
that you think have a nice set.

00:12:21.696 --> 00:12:23.643
-Okay, well, let's start
at the running-back position,

00:12:23.643 --> 00:12:26.039
because this guy here,
a rookie

00:12:26.039 --> 00:12:27.515
Saquon Barkley. 

00:12:27.515 --> 00:12:28.699
I mean, this is the most
unbelievable lower half

00:12:28.699 --> 00:12:30.987
I've ever seen
in a running back, maybe.

00:12:30.987 --> 00:12:33.080
I mean,
his nickname is "Saquad,"

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:34.345
and he's been there
for a few months.

00:12:34.345 --> 00:12:38.466
And, Lefkoe, have you ever seen
anybody bounce outside

00:12:38.466 --> 00:12:41.256
on Jacksonville Jaguars
and run for a 68-yard touchdown?

00:12:41.272 --> 00:12:42.830
That doesn't happen.

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:46.010
That's because he has
an unbelievable legs and ass.

00:12:46.026 --> 00:12:47.892
-Let's go to defense. Bosa.
-Oh.

00:12:47.892 --> 00:12:50.763
Joey Bosa is incredible
lower half.

00:12:50.763 --> 00:12:53.245
You want to know why he's one
of the best speed pass rushers

00:12:53.245 --> 00:12:56.182
in football, power pass rushers?

00:12:56.182 --> 00:12:58.842
He can do it all.
He's awesome in run blocking.

00:12:58.842 --> 00:13:00.854
So, yes, another guy...
-It all stems from the stems.

00:13:00.854 --> 00:13:02.898
-It all stems from down there.

00:13:03.126 --> 00:13:04.715
-Let's go to a rookie.
-Rookie.

00:13:04.732 --> 00:13:05.432
-Daron Payne.

00:13:05.448 --> 00:13:06.407
-So impressive.
The first time --

00:13:06.407 --> 00:13:08.366
-Not a lot of people
know this guy.

00:13:08.369 --> 00:13:10.313
-Daron Payne is going to be
one of the best interior

00:13:10.313 --> 00:13:13.736
defensive linemen in this sport,
and you can see right there.

00:13:13.736 --> 00:13:14.920
-Look at that.
-I mean, it looks like

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:16.607
he's got a turkey in there.

00:13:16.607 --> 00:13:18.246
I don't know what that is,
but all I know is,

00:13:18.246 --> 00:13:20.858
I turned on the
first preseason game

00:13:20.858 --> 00:13:23.145
when he was playing the Jets
and things like that.

00:13:23.145 --> 00:13:25.968
I said, "Who is this guy
with this legs and butt,

00:13:25.968 --> 00:13:28.029
and who is creating havoc
all over the field?"

00:13:28.029 --> 00:13:29.813
-Yeah.
-Oh, it's 95, Daron Payne.

00:13:29.813 --> 00:13:31.577
Watch out for him.
-Look out for him this season.

00:13:31.677 --> 00:13:33.074
A guy at a similar position

00:13:33.078 --> 00:13:35.118
that's been doing it
for a while --

00:13:35.118 --> 00:13:37.373
this is not just legs and ass --
this is legs and high ass.

00:13:37.373 --> 00:13:38.931
-Oh, this is a --
It's a little different.

00:13:38.931 --> 00:13:41.624
-Stephon Tuitt.
-It's nuanced here, yes.

00:13:41.624 --> 00:13:44.382
His high ass is what's
very impressive to me.

00:13:44.382 --> 00:13:46.781
Look how comfortable he is
in that position

00:13:46.787 --> 00:13:48.944
and he has a freaky lower half.

00:13:49.528 --> 00:13:50.709
-And, then, the last guy --

00:13:50.709 --> 00:13:53.013
I didn't expect a quarterback
to be on this list.

00:13:53.013 --> 00:13:54.359
-It's really rare.
-But if you always wonder,

00:13:54.359 --> 00:13:56.079
"Why is Russell Wilson
breaking tackles?"

00:13:56.079 --> 00:13:58.042
-Right.
-Probably 'cause of his legs.

00:13:58.056 --> 00:13:59.357
-And running around everywhere.

00:13:59.437 --> 00:14:01.400
We saw him in training camp
a few years ago.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:04.710
We know he probably has some
inner-thigh chaffage there,

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:06.706
but it's unbelievable
to see a quarterback

00:14:06.706 --> 00:14:08.620
with that type of lower half.

00:14:08.685 --> 00:14:10.534
It's really rare
for that position,

00:14:10.534 --> 00:14:11.929
but he does make the list.

00:14:11.929 --> 00:14:15.580
He can make some unbelievable
plays with his legs.

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:16.797
-Yes.
-It's about as good as I've

00:14:16.797 --> 00:14:18.597
ever seen at the
quarterback position.

00:14:18.597 --> 00:14:21.664
-So, if you want to steal Simms'
idea, look at legs and ass.

00:14:21.664 --> 00:14:24.113
One guy that's stolen
a lot from you...

00:14:24.113 --> 00:14:25.671
-Oh, my gosh.
-...DeAngelo Hall.

00:14:25.671 --> 00:14:27.001
-Oh, he -- I mean, every time
he was on the field,

00:14:27.001 --> 00:14:28.364
I felt like I threw
an interception to him.

00:14:28.371 --> 00:14:30.262
I threw a lot of
interceptions, though.

00:14:30.262 --> 00:14:32.258
-Well, look, he's amazing,

00:14:32.258 --> 00:14:35.421
and he's got an amazing story
about a little trip to Vegas.

00:14:36.541 --> 00:14:37.984
Rookie hazing
is always legendary.

00:14:37.984 --> 00:14:39.834
-Yes.
-I can't imagine what it's like

00:14:39.850 --> 00:14:41.391
when you're the eighth pick
in the draft.

00:14:41.391 --> 00:14:43.760
-They feel like
you got money to burn, man.

00:14:43.776 --> 00:14:46.794
So, they tell I have to take
them to Vegas -- rookie hazing.

00:14:46.794 --> 00:14:49.574
I'm like, "All right, cool."
I had never been to Vegas.

00:14:49.577 --> 00:14:50.864
And one night, we pick a club.

00:14:50.864 --> 00:14:53.089
You know, it's DB night.
I'm the rookie.

00:14:53.089 --> 00:14:55.458
I got to pay for everything.
-Yeah.

00:14:55.458 --> 00:14:57.015
-And I was kind of
looking at the bottles

00:14:57.015 --> 00:14:59.362
that we were anticipating
on buying,

00:14:59.362 --> 00:15:01.308
and I'm looking like, "Whew.

00:15:01.308 --> 00:15:04.000
Man, I didn't know that bottle
cost that much," you know?

00:15:04.011 --> 00:15:06.847
I was kind of doing that,
but as the night wore on, man,

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:08.451
it was --
it was bigger than that.

00:15:08.905 --> 00:15:11.485
On one side of our table
is Paris Hilton

00:15:11.485 --> 00:15:13.621
and her crew of lovely ladies.
-Right.

00:15:13.621 --> 00:15:15.845
-On the other side
is David Hasselhoff

00:15:15.845 --> 00:15:19.772
and his crew
of lovely ladies, as well.

00:15:19.776 --> 00:15:21.735
He had on a black
kind of shiny shirt,

00:15:21.743 --> 00:15:23.682
unbuttoned to probably
his belly button --

00:15:23.682 --> 00:15:25.483
you know, vintage
David Hasselhoff gear

00:15:25.483 --> 00:15:27.746
that he probably
would still wear.

00:15:27.754 --> 00:15:29.344
-Right. Right.
-I had never met

00:15:29.351 --> 00:15:31.616
another celebrity
besides other athletes.

00:15:31.616 --> 00:15:34.503
I was, without a doubt, shocked.

00:15:34.503 --> 00:15:36.726
And like I said, man,
Hasselhoff had a --

00:15:36.734 --> 00:15:39.451
His crew might have been better
than Paris' crew.

00:15:39.457 --> 00:15:40.749
Our table --

00:15:40.749 --> 00:15:43.848
looking like we probably ain't
even supposed to be in there.

00:15:43.848 --> 00:15:45.770
We don't even have no girls
over there with us,

00:15:45.778 --> 00:15:48.463
so we're all just kind of
partying at their tables.

00:15:48.469 --> 00:15:52.122
Initially, they had joked
about me having to pay

00:15:52.174 --> 00:15:54.036
for all the tabs,
the three -- those tables,

00:15:54.040 --> 00:15:55.732
'cuz we were
at all the tables.

00:15:55.732 --> 00:15:57.638
They're like,
"Man, we can't let them go.

00:15:57.638 --> 00:15:58.685
We drunk all their liquor."

00:15:58.685 --> 00:16:01.102
I'm like, "Well, somebody
got to pay for that.

00:16:01.150 --> 00:16:02.854
I got our table."

00:16:02.854 --> 00:16:06.812
Our bill ended up being
around 15 grand or so.

00:16:06.812 --> 00:16:09.181
And before I could
even put my card down,

00:16:09.181 --> 00:16:11.355
Paris had took my bill...
-Oh, my gosh. What?

00:16:11.355 --> 00:16:13.326
-...and paid my tab.
-Damn!

00:16:13.334 --> 00:16:14.770
Well, she should've.
She's a Hilton.

00:16:14.770 --> 00:16:17.601
-Hey, she had the most money
in the room, without a doubt.

00:16:17.609 --> 00:16:19.523
But I always tell people that
story, and they think I'm lying.

00:16:19.523 --> 00:16:22.703
But I promise you, man.
it's the damnedest thing.

00:16:22.703 --> 00:16:24.317
I wish I could meet her
and say, "Thank you."

00:16:24.317 --> 00:16:24.917
-So you're a big
Paris Hilton fan.

00:16:25.017 --> 00:16:27.116
-I'm a Paris Hilton fan, man,
'cause she took over my check.

00:16:27.119 --> 00:16:30.189
-So you got an extra $15,000.
-I kind of got off unscathed.

00:16:31.740 --> 00:16:33.517
-It is time for
"Somebody &amp; Lefkoe."

00:16:33.517 --> 00:16:34.734
Bleacher Report gives me money

00:16:34.734 --> 00:16:36.942
to bet on
'Thursday Night Football',

00:16:36.942 --> 00:16:39.246
but I let random New Yorkers
pick the team.

00:16:39.246 --> 00:16:41.517
They win, and I got to pay them,

00:16:41.517 --> 00:16:43.853
and that's why we got to see
my good friend Allie.

00:16:46.465 --> 00:16:48.527
We are back in the newsstand,
and that means

00:16:48.527 --> 00:16:51.073
that the Eagles
have covered the spread,

00:16:51.138 --> 00:16:54.820
and my main man Allie
has won himself some money.

00:16:54.878 --> 00:16:56.702
-Yeah!
-Congratulations.

00:16:56.702 --> 00:16:59.720
Here is $113...
-Yes.

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:00.989
-...and sixty --
I dropped a dime.

00:17:01.164 --> 00:17:02.780
-Don't worry about it.
-...65 cents.

00:17:02.790 --> 00:17:05.879
I've done the math.
That is 90 packs of Starbursts.

00:17:05.879 --> 00:17:07.210
-[ laughs ] I like it.
-I don't think you're gonna

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:08.978
use your money there.
-No.

00:17:08.978 --> 00:17:11.330
-You gonna take the lady out
for a nice dinner?

00:17:11.330 --> 00:17:12.515
-No, no, no,
I'm gonna let it build up

00:17:12.515 --> 00:17:15.970
over the next couple of weeks.
-What? Next couple of weeks?

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:18.096
I like what he's saying.
All right.

00:17:18.096 --> 00:17:22.443
This week, it is the Ravens,
who just steamrolled the Bills,

00:17:22.460 --> 00:17:24.812
at the Bengals,
who hung on to beat the Colts.

00:17:24.812 --> 00:17:28.235
And the Ravens are favored
by 1 in Cincinnati.

00:17:28.235 --> 00:17:30.863
Take me through your mind-set.
-I don't know.

00:17:30.874 --> 00:17:33.135
I like --
The last couple of matchups,

00:17:33.135 --> 00:17:34.692
the Bengals have beat them,
what, 5 out of 6?

00:17:34.692 --> 00:17:36.104
-5 of the last 6 times
in Cincinnati.

00:17:36.104 --> 00:17:37.369
-In Cincinnati.
-He knows his stuff.

00:17:37.369 --> 00:17:40.598
-A home game. Prime time.
-Yeah.

00:17:40.598 --> 00:17:42.269
-I like the Bengals.
-Really?

00:17:42.283 --> 00:17:44.605
-Going with the Bengals.
-Wow!

00:17:44.605 --> 00:17:46.552
Even after the Ravens
looked amazing in Week 1.

